WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:11.600
AI agents taking on enterprise procurement, why airlines stopped hedging fuel, what's driving beef prices, and how creators are building beyond the platforms.

2
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.320
For clips for industries being reshaped by complexity and risk.

3
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:32.960
On the A16Z podcast, why AI agents can beat the incumbents breaks down how procurement decisions really happen in stakeholder meetings, emails, spreadsheets, and negotiations that never reach the ERP.

4
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:43.280
You'll hear why retrieval, process, policy, and principal agents could turn that hidden work into an opening for AI native companies.

5
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:53.040
So like there are probably like 30 stakeholder meetings happened, 500 emails, 20 Excel sheets, and that's the same for the counterpart procurement, right?

6
00:00:53.040 --> 00:01:03.200
So you see in your ERP system 8K for aluminum, but you don't see that maybe the supplier did like a pushback and asked for like 10K.

7
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:10.720
You don't see that like a cost engineer had run three weeks of Excel sheets and 3D modeling to find out the prices of the pot and everything else.

8
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.080
So that's what we see.

9
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.240
So like most of the work in procurement actually happens outside of this ERP or any system of record.

10
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.040
Yeah.

11
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:27.520
And I'm sure in the workflow that you described, an agent can do a huge number of things, and different kinds of agents can do a large number of things too.

12
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:39.520
And I actually think that's a good bridge into the next question, which is you know, a year ago, I think a lot of the incumbents were releasing chat bots, and that was kind of the extent of what you would see.

13
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.920
But Sima, in this piece that you wrote, you lay out four different kinds of agents.

14
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.120
Retrieval agents, which are kind of the chat bots that we're familiar with, process agents, policy agents, and principal agents.

15
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:59.440
So can you just walk us through all of them and explain kind of what changes in the kind of judgment that's necessary across all of them?

16
00:01:59.440 --> 00:01:59.840
Yeah.

17
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:13.560
So a year ago I made this meme, which was the slap on a chat bot strategy, which is essentially like all the incumbents effectively had a chat bot that sat on top of the system record, which you could chat with to retrieve information, maybe do some like analytics.

18
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.400
And that really was in that first bucket of what the retrieval agent is.

19
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:27.320
And maybe let me walk you through an example of what each of like the retrieval, the process, the policy, and the principle back to the customer support example, just because it's very it's easier to understand.

20
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:35.640
So imagine if you're a customer and there's a service outage and you're calling in to say, hey, I want to get compensated for this service outage.

21
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:47.000
The retrieval assistant, which the incumbent may have, is going to be able to pull up, yes, this was what the contract term said, and yes, there was an outage, and just verify that information.

22
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:54.600
It's pulling up information about the customer, it's in the database, and it's the system of record has the final number.

23
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.160
The competitive intelligence lives in everything that came before it.

24
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:07.720
Odd lots asks a former airline treasury and risk management executive how carriers protect themselves from higher fuel prices.

25
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:19.000
Many US airlines have pulled back from hedging, making ticket pricing, not financial contracts, a bigger part of how they pass along fuel shocks.

26
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.880
Two and a half to yeah, nearly three years.

27
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.600
Got it.

28
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:29.800
So former group treasurer, what is the basic expectation of that role within the airline?

29
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.360
What is uh what is the obligation of the treasurer to do for the company?

30
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.240
The actual title is vice president, treasury and risk management.

31
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:39.880
Okay.

32
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:47.120
So basically, I do the treasury and I do the risk management for all financial products, right, of the airline.

33
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.640
That's what a treasurer does.

34
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.280
So I protect the airline financially.

35
00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.120
So who actually hedges?

36
00:03:53.120 --> 00:04:00.160
Because my understanding is that some of the US carriers weren't as prevalent when it comes to hedging as they had been in the past.

37
00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.800
Well, I think the US carriers aren't as prevalent.

38
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.040
It's because right, they got smacked in 2014 and 2015.

39
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:13.040
So they realized, right, Delta recently, 2020 already lost nearly over a billion dollars.

40
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.520
So they were still hedging as well.

41
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:27.440
But most of the American carriers, especially the small ones, especially the smaller airlines, they just put everything into the surge into the surcharge to pass all the costs on to the customer.

42
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.200
And that's built into the ticket.

43
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.360
Now you will never see a ticket that tells you the percentage of your surcharge.

44
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.480
It will just tell you it's a fuel surcharge.

45
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.360
And then that's it.

46
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.440
And some of them even have done away with that.

47
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.880
They say that's a ticket price, you pay it and you fly.

48
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:43.680
Then that's it.

49
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.280
Don't worry about the fuel.

50
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.560
You want to get somewhere?

51
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.600
Take you.

52
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.400
So in that aspect, that's when some of the US carriers actually dropped out, right?

53
00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:01.840
Because they don't have a hedge book big enough, or rather, they don't have a balance sheet big enough to maybe take on these risks of hedging.

54
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.000
And that's what the international carriers do have is they have a balance sheet.

55
00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.040
And they have a hedge book that's big enough.

56
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.480
Plus, they have a revenue book that's big enough as well.

57
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:18.880
And that's why they can charge the surcharge at the same time as they're hedging the fuel on the consumption side.

58
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.520
So think of an airline.

59
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.280
Very similar to an oil refinery.

60
00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.800
Okay, so uh what's an oil refinery?

61
00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.960
Less hedging means travelers can feel an oil price spike much sooner.

62
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:37.000
Here's why your hamburger just got so pricey.

63
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.120
Freakonomics Radio's episode.

64
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:45.080
Follows B from the way a carcass is cut to the coordination of cattle production across regions in multiple years.

65
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:56.520
The key constraint, unlike a factory input, cattle supply is biological, geographically dispersed, and slow to adjust when prices jump.

66
00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:57.160
Animal.

67
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.200
After the middle meats, the tenderloin, and so on.

68
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.960
This one falls right behind that.

69
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:08.040
But we never recognized it for years because we always just kind of cut across it with everything else, and it wound up in a roast.

70
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:13.000
If you fabricate it a little bit differently, separate it out and get that piece of meat by itself.

71
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.160
Hey, we've got an excellent new steak here.

72
00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:25.480
The other thing about cattle that makes them unique to any other market is their ruminants, so they have the ability to utilize a wide range of feedstuffs.

73
00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.040
We can use lots of grain and a little bit of forage.

74
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.680
We can use lots of forage and a little bit of grain.

75
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.240
We can change the timing of these animals.

76
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.400
We've got producers all over the country producing these calves.

77
00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.880
That's really tied to wherever there's grass.

78
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.800
And then they go to a feed lot, oftentimes in a different part of the country.

79
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:51.960
I visited uh a feedlot in Pennsylvania a few years ago, and I was in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, but that's not very far from Hershey, Pennsylvania.

80
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.600
And then as we were driving by the feed bins, I looked out the window and I said, that's very colorful.

81
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:57.720
What's in those feed bins?

82
00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.880
And the guy said, Well, that one is Reese's pieces, and uh no.

83
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:02.680
You're kidding.

84
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.160
You know, it the crumbles from you know, candy manufacturing, cattle can utilize that.

85
00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:13.080
You have to be careful nutritionally, obviously, you can't just feed them nothing but that, but that stuff can all be utilized.

86
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.240
All of this takes place over from beginning to end.

87
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:22.160
So I call it the miracle of the beef industry.

88
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.440
We're talking 24 months at least.

89
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:34.880
You walk into a grocery store any day of the year, and there's a piece of fresh meat in there that's probably was a live animal no more than about three weeks ago.

90
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:45.840
But the process that led to this piece of meat being available to you today started two and a half years ago or more with some guy or several people along the way that don't know each other.

91
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.840
And you know, how does it work?

92
00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:56.080
I am sorry to interrupt Darrell Peel's excellent storytelling here, but I want to highlight a larger point he's making.

93
00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.600
How does it all work?

94
00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:03.280
The miracle of the beef industry is really the miracle of any industry.

95
00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.080
Today's beef price can reflect production decisions made years ago.

96
